+You are the data and backend engineer `fix-data-3` for melon-seek (repo /home/user/melon-seek, branch claude/stoic-ride-54ddxp), a zero-build vanilla ES-module app, "the Zillow of job postings". You are in your own git worktree. First run `git fetch origin claude/stoic-ride-54ddxp && git checkout --detach origin/claude/stoic-ride-54ddxp` (head f53a270 or later). Read docs/CONTRACT.md, docs/DATA_SOURCES.md (§7a norm-11), docs/process/PLAYBOOK.md (including "Checkpoints" and "Known traps") and docs/process/TEMPLATE.md.
+
+**Standing rules from the user** (never break them):
+- "we must present them as they are and not cut anything". Never drop, merge away or hide a posting or a posted pay row.
+- Present pay as posted, so we don't misrepresent the companies and roles. Big-tech figures are base pay only, and say so.
+- Respectful crawling: honest UA, about 1 request/s, robots.txt. You crawl nothing in this lane; work from the stored snapshots.
+
+**Your findings** (evidence in docs/QA.md's last two sections, docs/process/qa-desktop-beta-3.md, docs/process/methodology-review-beta-2.md, docs/process/ux-review-beta-2.md and docs/process/lead.md):
+1. **D-38 (Medium).** Google "Fellow, Core AI Foundations" (google:84245035704296134) is a Google Fellow, the top engineering level, posted "US: $550,000 (USD) + 80% bonus target". It is levelled Intern, its pay is quarantined as "implausible for an intern/fellow/resident title", and it shows a "$138K" estimate. Cause: server/vet.js `JUNIOR_RE` matches "fellow" and `NOT_JUNIOR_RE` spares only technical/distinguished/senior/principal/research fellow. A title that is a Fellow level (not "Fellowship", "Fellows Program", "AI Residency") must not be junior, and its posted pay must show as posted. Audit every board for other titles the junior rule quarantines wrongly, and list them.
+2. **MR-28 (High).** Minimums and shift-inclusive pay are called "posted base pay" and counted as base pay:
+   - scripts/build-market.js companyPart (l.109–143) pools Amazon's 285 stated minimums ("from €46K"), 636 hourly figures that include "the highest available shift differential", and part-time hourly pay (Amazon 83, Apple 40) under "full-time roles";
+   - Juice treats them as gross base pay.
+   Fix it as methodology-review-beta-2.md §MR-28 says:
+   - give minimums, shift-inclusive and part-time their own groups, or leave them out of the base-pay cells, and count them on the card;
+   - add ONE line in the drawer's Juice block, "Gross pay is the posted minimum" or "…includes the highest shift differential". It must fit one line on phones (fix-density rule, test/e2e/phone-sizes.mjs). fix-phone-text owns the other drawer lines, so keep your edit to this one line and tell the lead.
+3. **No posted pay at a place without its own row** (lead decision 2026-10-16, docs/process/lead.md "salary-audit-backend, final"). A place the posting lists but gives no pay row of its own has no posted pay there: no Juice for that place, and no scoring on another place's row. The card still shows the posting's range. This affects 7 Amazon postings; list them. fix-pay-at-place's `payAt(job, place)` (public/features/shared.js, being integrated now) is the place to apply it: return no pay for a place with no row, rather than the headline range.
+4. **Places:**
+   - D-41: "St.", "St" and "Saint" spellings make separate Location labels, with no state. Examples: Amazon "St. Louis" (MO, 94) and "St Louis" (MI, 80); Google "Saint George" (15) and "St George" (15), both St. George, SC, next to "St. George" (14, UT). Canonicalize in server/geo.js, and disambiguate by state where two places share a name.
+   - D-42: NVIDIA's state-only places read like cities: "New York, US" (the state, 38) beside "New York" (187), "Washington, US" (the state, 82) beside "Washington, DC" (151). Label a region-only place as the region ("New York State", "Washington State").
+   - V-45: 67 Amazon postings at "Cajamar, Sao Paulo, BRA" are pinned and named as São Paulo city, 33 km away, with precision 'city'; "Luxembourg, LUX" (121) sits at the country centroid with no approximate mark (map.js `isApprox` only flags 'centroid').
+   - Amazon splits "Town and Country, MO" into two places.
+   - An old link to "Columbus, OH" finds nothing on a board that now labels the place "Columbus". Resolve old location keys to the current label.
+5. **UX-32 (round 2, still open):** NVIDIA's inverted department names ("Management, Product Development", "Management, Hardware (Engineering)"), "Intern" as a department, and Amazon's raw "--" in department names ("Project/Program/Product Management--Technical"). Show clean, natural names, and keep the full original name reachable (the filter still matches it). The display must still distinguish Amazon's "…--Technical" from "…--Non-Tech".
+
+**Your job:**
+1. Write a failing test for each item first: unit tests in test/fix-data-3.test.js, plus e2e on the built site in test/e2e/fix-data-3.e2e.js (registered in scripts/e2e.js) where the result is visible. The reviewers' red tests for D-38, D-41, D-42 and V-45 are in test/e2e/beta-desktop-qa-3.e2e.js and test/e2e/beta-viz-review-2.e2e.js; they must pass after your fix.
+2. Fix, keeping every posting and pay row. Show before/after counts per board (postings, pay rows, places) and prove nothing was dropped.
+3. If the normalizer changes, bump NORMALIZER_VERSION once and re-baseline the golden files with the tooling (never by hand). Say how many golden hashes changed and why.
+4. Update docs/DATA_SOURCES.md and docs/process/methodology-review-beta-2.md (mark MR-28 fixed) where the rules change.
